# Mobile CI/CD Overview & Developer Journey

This section explains how an Android, iOS, or React Native application moves from prepared source to **verified availability for its intended users**. The journey includes secret-free enrollment, protected provisioning, Bitrise workflows, quality/security gates, signing, distribution, and recovery.

Use this overview to understand the process and responsibilities. The following pages supply the implementation YAML, scripts, platform configuration, and verification steps.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Keep company source, configuration, credentials, signing material, and operational evidence within approved systems. Redact sensitive identifiers and logs before sharing. Never place secrets, private artifact download links, or unredacted screenshots in reusable examples, PR descriptions, or AI prompts.

## 1. Begin with the right journey

| Your situation | What to do |
| :--- | :--- |
| **New application** | Prepare its source, submit a secret-free enrollment request, and follow validation, review, protected promotion, provisioning, and route verification. |
| **Existing enrolled application** | Use its established platform identity and approved build/release triggers. Do not submit another create-only request. |
| **Failed or uncertain provisioning/delivery** | Preserve resources and evidence, then follow reconciliation and reviewed recovery. Do not repeat enrollment or upload blindly. |

**Prerequisite:** complete [Machine Setup](/mobile/onboarding/machine-setup-guide/) and [First Local Run](/mobile/onboarding/first-local-run/) for your assigned stack. CI/CD repository preparation adds build-platform requirements; it does not repeat workstation installation.

### Three terms to distinguish

- **Continuous integration:** validate proposed source changes through the required review, test, analysis, and build checks.
- **Continuous delivery/deployment:** move eligible artifacts through the applicable signing, assessment, approval, and distribution process.
- **GitOps enrollment:** provision a new application through reviewed declarative configuration and authorized platform automation.

Enrollment is not a step to repeat for every feature. Automation does not bypass release approval or destination processing.

