## 5. Separate validation from live apply

The workshop establishes this configuration-repository journey: request PR to `develop`, validation/review, promotion PR from `develop` to protected `main`, then authorized live apply from the protected merge.

<MermaidZoom title="Governed enrollment: previews do not provision a live app">

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor AUTHOR as Request Author
    participant CONFIG as Configuration Repository
    participant VELA as Vela Validation
    actor PLATFORM as Platform Engineering
    participant APPLY as Live Apply Worker
    participant SERVICES as Bitrise and Distribution Services

    AUTHOR->>CONFIG: Submit secret-free request PR to develop
    CONFIG->>VELA: Validate exact PR revision
    VELA-->>CONFIG: Return checks and sanitized preview
    Note over VELA,APPLY: Local validation and PR preview do not create an app
    AUTHOR->>CONFIG: Complete reviewed request merge to develop
    PLATFORM->>CONFIG: Review promotion and complete protected main merge
    CONFIG->>APPLY: Start authorized live apply for merge commit
    APPLY->>SERVICES: Provision new app and approved bindings
    SERVICES-->>APPLY: Return identities and operation outcomes
    APPLY-->>PLATFORM: Report status and per-route evidence
```

</MermaidZoom>

Follow the required repository approvals and checks at each boundary. An administrative bypass does not turn pending checks into passing checks.

Keep these identities separate:

- **Application source commit:** the application code to build.
- **Request PR head commit:** the configuration revision reviewed and validated.
- **Protected configuration merge commit:** the revision that triggered live apply.
- **Build and correlation identities:** the execution evidence linking requests, resources, and outcomes.

A schema-validation pass does not prove provider access. A successful protected merge does not prove successful provisioning. Inspect the actual apply result.

## 6. Track four governed QA/UAT routes

| Route | Platform | Intended purpose | Required outcome evidence |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `qa_android` | Android | Private QA delivery. | Artifact identity, applicable signing, destination state, and intended QA visibility. |
| `qa_ios` | iOS | Private QA IPA delivery. | Artifact identity, required signing, destination state, and intended QA visibility. |
| `uat_android` | Android | Approved business acceptance delivery. | Applicable approvals, artifact/version, destination state, and intended UAT visibility. |
| `uat_ios` | iOS | Approved business acceptance IPA delivery. | Applicable approvals, artifact/version, required signing, and intended UAT visibility. |

The approved catalog defines applicability, triggers, artifact format, and destination. React Native requires independent Android and iOS verification. Record native-only applicability accurately; do not report unsupported routes as successful.

These four routes do not automatically authorize public production publication. Store delivery follows its separate approved destination and release process.

## 7. Read success claims precisely

| Observed result | What you can conclude | What remains separate |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Local validation passed** | The supplied input passed those validation rules. | Provider access and resource creation. |
| **PR checks passed** | The identified revision passed configured checks. | Protected promotion and live apply. |
| **App resource exists** | The identified resource was created or found. | Correct bindings, builds, and publication. |
| **Bitrise build passed** | The workflow completed its configured work. | Downstream signing, enablement, assignment, and user visibility. |
| **Upload confirmed** | Destination accepted the identified upload. | Processing, review, publication, and audience access. |
| **Published and user-verified** | The exact version is available through the intended route to the intended audience. | Installation and successful launch unless separately tested. |

### Build signing and distribution signing are separate

The internal App Management path can include signing after Bitrise produces the artifact. A passed iOS build therefore does not establish successful App Management signing or publication.

### Use evidence-backed route outcomes

Record **not started**, **build passed**, **published and verified**, **failed**, or **unknown** as human-readable distinctions. Use actual platform schema values in machine-readable records rather than inventing enum names from this list.

