# Bitrise Pipelines & Four Governed Build Routes

An enrolled application uses an approved, versioned workflow template. The template defines how source is checked out, dependencies are prepared, checks execute, artifacts are produced, and eligible outputs move toward distribution.

**Outcome:** you can identify the route and source being executed, understand dependencies and parallel work, and inspect actual results without treating a green pipeline as proof that every test or delivery step ran.

Complete [Enrollment, Validation & Promotion](../gitops-enrollment/) first. Existing applications reuse their enrolled Bitrise identity.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Keep workflow configuration and evidence in approved systems. Do not expose credentials, signing paths containing sensitive information, private artifact URLs, or full environment dumps. Examples are configuration patterns, not authorization to edit platform-owned templates.

## 1. Respect template ownership

| Application developer | Platform owner |
| :--- | :--- |
| Prepare source, tests, scripts, native targets, and documented template inputs. | Maintain approved workflow definitions, immutable releases, protected bindings, and permitted extension points. |
| Inspect the exact route, source revision, logs, and artifacts. | Review template updates, runner configuration, triggers, and integration contracts. |
| Propose a required change through review. | Publish and authorize the approved change. |

Do not replace the attached workflow with this page's examples or manually create a competing Bitrise app. A YAML snippet explains a mechanism; it is not the company's complete approved release template.

The original pipeline draft used `pr-checks`, `deploy-dev`, `deploy-staging-qa`, and `release-prod`. The later supplied governed process defines **four QA/UAT route identifiers** instead. Use those authoritative identifiers below; do not silently map old branches, Firebase/Slack destinations, or production triggers onto them.

## 2. Understand the configuration model

| Element | Purpose |
| :--- | :--- |
| `format_version` | Configuration format supported by the approved template and CLI. |
| `app.envs` | Non-secret defaults supplied under the template's input contract. |
| `trigger_map` | Configured event routing where this trigger mechanism is used. |
| `pipelines` | Orchestration of multiple workflows. |
| `stages` | Sequential groups in the supplied stage-based model. Workflows in a stage may run concurrently. |
| `workflows` | Ordered collections of steps. |
| `steps` | Individual actions with inputs, outputs, conditions, and failure behavior. |

### Stage-based templates and graph pipelines

The supplied platform model is `pipelines → stages → workflows → steps`. Keep that approved template model unless its owner publishes a change.

Upstream Bitrise also supports graph pipelines using workflow dependencies and recommends that model for new upstream configurations. A pipeline cannot mix stage orchestration and graph dependencies. This public product guidance does not authorize an internal template migration. See [Bitrise pipeline configuration](https://docs.bitrise.io/en/bitrise-ci/workflows-and-pipelines/build-pipelines/configuring-a-bitrise-pipeline).

<MermaidZoom title="Approved route execution: orchestration, isolated runners, and evidence handoff">

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    SOURCE["Identified source commit<br/>Approved template and inputs"] --> PREPARE["Preparation stage<br/>Validate inputs and determine scope"]
    PREPARE --> ANDROID["Android workflows<br/>Compatible Linux or macOS runner"]
    PREPARE --> IOS["iOS workflows<br/>Compatible macOS and Xcode runner"]
    ANDROID --> ANDROIDOUT["Android reports and artifacts"]
    IOS --> IOSOUT["iOS reports and artifacts"]
    ANDROIDOUT --> GATE["Required evidence gates<br/>Evaluate actual execution and outcomes"]
    IOSOUT --> GATE
    GATE --> DELIVERY["Eligible downstream delivery<br/>Separate route and audience verification"]
