## 3. Preserve the supplied PR rules

| Rule | Supplied requirement and implementation direction |
| :--- | :--- |
| Description | A meaningful body with a minimum character requirement. The Danger source uses 15; the recording conversation log mentions 20. Resolve this conflict with the owner before enforcing a numeric policy. |
| Assignee | Required by the later governance instructions. The old `warn()` implementation did not enforce that requirement; use failure for absence. |
| Reviewer | A designated peer/team is required. After a review is submitted, an empty pending-reviewer list must not erase that evidence. |
| Draft/WIP | Block ready-to-merge eligibility for draft or the supplied `[WIP]`, `WIP:`, and `(WIP)` markers. |
| Large diff | Warn when additions plus deletions exceed 500 lines, as supplied. This is not an automatic size-based merge prohibition. |
| Tests changed | A source/test diff heuristic can prompt review. It is not coverage measurement and does not prove whether existing tests cover the change. |
| Lockfile | The supplied npm rule requires `package-lock.json` changes alongside `package.json` changes. It is intentionally strict and can flag script-only edits; changes to that policy require owner review. |

Do not replace two-approval branch protection with a naive count of historical review objects. Dismissed reviews, changed commits, reviewer eligibility, and outstanding requested changes belong to the source platform's configured review enforcement.

## 4. Implement testable Danger rules

Keep the rules and wrapper in the **trusted review tooling checkout**. Changes to these files must be reviewed before activation; a PR must not be able to weaken the rule that evaluates itself.

### Pure policy evaluator

**File: `ci/review/prPolicy.ts`**

```typescript
export type PrInput = Readonly<{
  body: string;
  title: string;
  draft: boolean;
  assigneeCount: number;
  hasDesignatedReviewer: boolean;
  additions: number;
  deletions: number;
  changedFiles: readonly string[];
}>;

export type PrPolicy = Readonly<{
  minimumDescriptionLength: number;
  lockfile: string;
}>;

export type ReviewFindings = Readonly<{
  failures: readonly string[];
  warnings: readonly string[];
}>;

export function evaluatePr(input: PrInput, policy: PrPolicy): ReviewFindings {
  if (!Number.isSafeInteger(policy.minimumDescriptionLength) ||
      policy.minimumDescriptionLength < 1) {
    throw new Error('Invalid trusted description policy');
  }
  if (!policy.lockfile) throw new Error('Missing trusted lockfile policy');
  const failures: string[] = [];
  const warnings: string[] = [];
  if (input.body.trim().length < policy.minimumDescriptionLength) {
    failures.push('Provide the required description with context and verification.');
  }
  if (input.assigneeCount < 1) failures.push('Assign the PR to its responsible owner.');
  if (!input.hasDesignatedReviewer) failures.push('Designate the required reviewer or team.');
  const wip = /\[wip\]|\(wip\)|^\s*wip:/i.test(input.title);
  if (input.draft || wip) failures.push('Complete draft/WIP work before merge.');
  if (input.additions + input.deletions > 500) {
    warnings.push('More than 500 changed lines: consider a smaller review slice.');
  }
  const files = new Set(input.changedFiles);
  const isTest = (file: string): boolean =>
    /(?:\.(?:test|spec)\.[^/]+$|(?:^|\/)__tests__\/)/.test(file) ||
    /(?:^|\/)(?:Tests|UITests|androidTest|test)\//.test(file);
  const sourceChanged = input.changedFiles.some(file =>
    /(?:\.(?:kt|java|swift|js|jsx|ts|tsx))$/.test(file) && !isTest(file));
  const testsChanged = input.changedFiles.some(isTest);
  if (sourceChanged && !testsChanged) {
    warnings.push('Source changed without test-file changes: explain regression coverage.');
  }
  if (files.has('package.json') && !files.has(policy.lockfile)) {
    failures.push('Update the project lockfile with package.json under the supplied rule.');
  }
  return { failures, warnings };
}
```

The evaluator assumes trusted metadata normalization. Deleted source/test files are included in `changedFiles`; this prevents a modified-files-only blind spot. The test heuristic is advisory because a correct change may use existing tests, and a changed test file may still provide no useful assertion.

### Danger wrapper

**File: `dangerfile.ts` in that trusted tooling checkout**

```typescript
import { danger, fail, warn, markdown } from 'danger';
import { evaluatePr } from './ci/review/prPolicy';

const pr = danger.github.pr;
const rawDraft: unknown = 'draft' in pr ? pr.draft : undefined;
const rawMinimum = process.env.PR_DESCRIPTION_MIN_LENGTH ?? '';
const minimum = /^\d+$/.test(rawMinimum) ? Number(rawMinimum) : NaN;

if (typeof rawDraft !== 'boolean' ||
    !Number.isSafeInteger(minimum) || minimum < 1) {
  fail('Required PR metadata or approved description policy is unavailable.');
} else {
  const requested = danger.github.requested_reviewers;
  const designated = requested.users.some(user => user.login !== pr.user.login) ||
    requested.teams.length > 0 ||
    danger.github.reviews.some(review =>
      review.user.login !== pr.user.login && review.user.type !== 'Bot');
  const findings = evaluatePr({
    body: pr.body ?? '',
    title: pr.title,
    draft: rawDraft,
    assigneeCount: pr.assignees?.length ?? (pr.assignee ? 1 : 0),
    hasDesignatedReviewer: designated,
    additions: pr.additions,
    deletions: pr.deletions,
    changedFiles: [
      ...danger.git.created_files,
      ...danger.git.modified_files,
      ...danger.git.deleted_files,
    ],
  }, {
    minimumDescriptionLength: minimum,
    lockfile: 'package-lock.json',
  });
  findings.failures.forEach(text => fail(text));
  findings.warnings.forEach(text => warn(text));
  markdown([
    '| PR policy result | Count |',
    '| :--- | ---: |',
    `| Failures | ${findings.failures.length} |`,
    `| Warnings | ${findings.warnings.length} |`,
  ].join('\n'));
}
```

`PR_DESCRIPTION_MIN_LENGTH` is an **example-owned trusted policy input**, not a built-in Danger variable. The owner must resolve 15 versus 20 and bind the chosen value. Missing policy fails rather than silently selecting a conflicting threshold.

This wrapper is npm-specific. Yarn/pnpm or native-only tooling must use the actual approved manifest/lock policy rather than fabricating `package-lock.json`. A designated-reviewer check is not proof of two approvals. Reevaluate on relevant metadata/review changes through the configured automation; a stale comment cannot enforce a later assignee or draft change.

The wrapper uses documented [Danger GitHub metadata](https://danger.systems/js/reference). Confirm its types and draft field against the pinned release and GitHub Enterprise payload. Missing metadata must fail inspection or be fetched through the trusted adapter, not default to eligible.

## 5. Run Danger across a trusted boundary

Use the approved pinned Danger dependency and lockfile in trusted tooling. The script contract is:

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "danger": "danger ci --failOnErrors"
  }
}
```

`--failOnErrors` makes reported rule failures affect the command exit result; branch protection must also require that status. Verify this behavior for the approved release. See [Danger CI setup](https://danger.systems/js/guides/getting_started.html).

**Bitrise step excerpt — requires an already prepared trusted checkout and PR context:**

```yaml
- script@1:
    title: Run trusted PR policy
    inputs:
      - content: |-
          #!/usr/bin/env bash
          set -euo pipefail
          : "${MOBILE_TRUSTED_REVIEW_DIR:?Trusted tooling checkout is required}"
          : "${PR_DESCRIPTION_MIN_LENGTH:?Approved policy input is required}"
          cd "$MOBILE_TRUSTED_REVIEW_DIR"
          npm run danger
```

`MOBILE_TRUSTED_REVIEW_DIR` is an example-owned input prepared by protected orchestration. The step alone is not a complete trust design.

### Credential and execution requirements

- Platform-owned automation binds `DANGER_GITHUB_API_TOKEN` and the supplied Enterprise host/API settings where needed. Do not ask developers to create broad personal tokens or paste them into Bitrise.
- Install and execute only reviewed tooling when that credential is present. A protected Dangerfile is insufficient if untrusted package scripts or dependencies run in the same credential-bearing process environment.
- Do not run PR-controlled `dangerfile.ts`, arbitrary npm lifecycle scripts, or shell helpers with the protected bot credential.
- Give source-build/test jobs only their approved access. Fork/untrusted PR handling needs an explicit supported path; do not expose secrets to make a check pass.
- Missing credentials, API failures, or unavailable PR context must not be converted into a passing required status.

