# Troubleshooting & Safe Recovery

Investigate the **exact failed operation**, not just the final red build status. A workflow can fail after it created an app, signed an artifact, uploaded a version, or completed another route successfully.

The supplied **one-write rule** is the recovery boundary: automation attempts an external mutation at most once in its normal execution. After a timeout or ambiguous response, stop, preserve state, and perform approved **GET-only reconciliation** before any further mutation is authorized.

**Outcome:** the failure is localized, completed work is preserved, the authoritative state is established as far as evidence permits, and the next action has an owner and reviewed authorization.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Keep incident evidence in approved systems. Do not paste tokens, signing material, private artifact/video URLs, personal data, raw API bodies, or unredacted logs into support messages. Use the approved Teams support channel with sanitized evidence and internal references.

## 1. Classify the failure before taking action

| Situation | What is known | Safe direction |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Local validation or compilation failure** | The identified local/check operation failed. | Diagnose source/configuration; verify no external mutation preceded it before rerunning a wider workflow. |
| **Confirmed external rejection** | A service returned an authoritative failure for the operation. | Establish whether any resource or partial side effect remains, then follow reviewed recovery. |
| **Partial failure** | Some required steps or routes completed and another failed. | Preserve completed resources and outcomes; reconcile only the unresolved scope. |
| **Unknown side effect** | Timeout, lost response, interrupted execution, or insufficient evidence. | Stop writes and perform read-only reconciliation. |
| **Destination still processing** | The submitted object exists but is not in its final required state. | Observe through the approved readback process; do not upload again merely because processing is incomplete. |
| **Security or authorization gate failed** | Required evidence or approval is absent/unacceptable. | Remediate or use the formal authorization process; do not lower thresholds or bypass the gate. |

Use actual machine-readable statuses from the platform contract. The supplied materials refer to `partial_failure` and `side_effect_unknown`; those labels do not replace investigation of completed steps.

## 2. Stop and preserve the evidence

Before pressing rerun, deleting a resource, replacing credentials, or replaying a merge:

1. Record the source and configuration revisions, workflow/route, execution identity, and correlation ID where provided.
2. Identify the first relevant failed operation and every completed mutation before it.
3. Preserve the Bitrise app, repository connection, artifacts, signing material, destination apps/versions, and successful route results.
4. Keep access controlled while retaining required logs/reports under the approved evidence policy.
5. Notify the responsible owner using a sanitized summary.

Do not preserve a leaked secret by copying it into another evidence bundle. Follow the credential incident procedure and retain only the approved incident reference and safe context.

### Safe local identity checks

Run only in the intended checkout. These commands inspect local Git state without fetching, switching branches, or changing external services:

```bash
git status --short --branch
git rev-parse --show-toplevel
git rev-parse HEAD
git branch --show-current
```

Paths and branch names may be confidential. Review the output before sharing it. The local HEAD does not prove which commit a remote build used; use the build's recorded source identity as well.

For an existing artifact you are authorized to inspect:

```bash
set -euo pipefail
: "${MOBILE_EXISTING_ARTIFACT:?Existing artifact path is required}"
test -s "$MOBILE_EXISTING_ARTIFACT"
shasum -a 256 "$MOBILE_EXISTING_ARTIFACT"
```

This computes a local hash; it does not prove that the destination received those bytes or that a later re-signing preserved them.

## 3. Reconcile before deciding on recovery

<MermaidZoom title="Recovery decision: preserve state and establish authoritative readback before another write">

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    FAILURE["Failure, timeout, or interruption"] --> STOP["Stop external writes<br/>Preserve identities and completed outcomes"]
    STOP --> CLASSIFY{"External mutation possible?"}
    CLASSIFY -->|No| LOCAL["Diagnose bounded local failure<br/>Rerun only reviewed non-mutating checks"]
    CLASSIFY -->|Yes or unknown| READ["Authorized GET-only reconciliation<br/>Resource, version, operation, and audit evidence"]
    READ --> RESULT{"Authoritative result?"}
    RESULT -->|Present or completed| ADOPT["Reconcile existing resource/result<br/>Do not create or upload again"]
    RESULT -->|Pending or inconclusive| WAIT["Keep unresolved state<br/>Follow bounded observation and escalation"]
    RESULT -->|Absence conclusively established| REVIEW["Obtain reviewed recovery decision<br/>Verify preconditions and authorization"]
    REVIEW --> ACTION["Execute only the authorized recovery scope<br/>Record and verify its outcome"]
    classDef failure fill:#881337,stroke:#fda4af,color:#ffffff,stroke-width:2px;
    classDef step fill:#312e81,stroke:#a5b4fc,color:#ffffff,stroke-width:2px;
    classDef decision fill:#78350f,stroke:#fcd34d,color:#ffffff,stroke-width:2px;
    classDef result fill:#064e3b,stroke:#6ee7b7,color:#ffffff,stroke-width:2px;
    class FAILURE,STOP failure;
    class READ,WAIT,REVIEW step;
    class CLASSIFY,RESULT decision;
    class LOCAL,ADOPT,ACTION result;
```

</MermaidZoom>

GET-only reconciliation means using the destination's **documented read-only operations** through the approved authenticated adapter. Do not invent an endpoint or assume a generic “retry” button is read-only.

### Establish enough evidence

| Readback question | Evidence to inspect |
| :--- | :--- |
| Did the app/resource get created? | Intended identity, owning workspace/environment, repository connection, and creation/apply correlation where available. |
| Did the artifact arrive? | Destination version/file reference, package identity, native version/build, and supported artifact association. |
| Is processing still running? | Operation/build status and any documented asynchronous processing state. |
| Did signing/enablement/assignment complete? | Actual operation results and current destination state. |
| Is the intended version visible? | Authorized user endpoint/catalog evidence for the intended audience. |

A single missing result can be inconclusive because of processing delays, permissions, filters, pagination, wrong environment, or eventual consistency. An inaccessible resource is not evidence that it does not exist.

Follow the provider's documented bounded observation and the internal reconciliation procedure. No universal delay or retry count is supplied here. Escalate unresolved state rather than polling indefinitely or repeating the mutation.

## 4. Make the recovery decision explicit

The supplied master guide permits controlled retry **after absence is established**. The workshop requires a reviewed recovery procedure and prohibits blind reruns. Apply both requirements: establish authoritative state first, then obtain authorization for the exact recovery action.

The following helper expresses a documentation-level decision. It does not perform readback, prove absence, authorize a user, or execute a retry.

**File: `ci/recovery/recoveryDecision.ts`**

```typescript
export type Readback =
  | { state: 'present'; authoritative: boolean; evidenceRef: string }
  | { state: 'pending'; evidenceRef: string }
  | { state: 'inconclusive'; evidenceRef: string }
  | {
      state: 'absent';
      authoritative: boolean;
      absenceConclusive: boolean;
      evidenceRef: string;
    };

export type RecoveryDecision =
  | 'preserve-and-reconcile'
  | 'use-existing-result'
  | 'observe-or-escalate'
  | 'request-reviewed-recovery'
  | 'follow-authorized-recovery';

export function recoveryDecision(
  readback: Readback | undefined,
  recoveryAuthorized: boolean,
): RecoveryDecision {
  if (!readback || readback.evidenceRef.trim().length === 0) {
    return 'preserve-and-reconcile';
  }
  switch (readback.state) {
    case 'pending':
    case 'inconclusive':
      return 'observe-or-escalate';
    case 'present':
      return readback.authoritative
        ? 'use-existing-result'
        : 'preserve-and-reconcile';
    case 'absent':
      if (!readback.authoritative || !readback.absenceConclusive) {
        return 'preserve-and-reconcile';
      }
      return recoveryAuthorized
        ? 'follow-authorized-recovery'
        : 'request-reviewed-recovery';
  }
}
```

`recoveryAuthorized` represents a decision already verified through the approved authority, not a command-line bypass. `absenceConclusive` must reflect the actual service contract and evidence. These fields are example-owned normalized inputs, not vendor response fields.

A present result still needs identity, completeness, and ownership checks before it is adopted. Authorization may prescribe an action other than repeating the original mutation. A newly uncertain recovery attempt must stop for reconciliation again; this helper is not a retry loop.

## 5. Diagnose the failing layer

| Symptom | Inspect first | Avoid |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Request validation fails** | Canonical path/case, filename/team agreement, schema, approved template/version, secret checks. | Creating another request directory or deleting validation rules. |
| **Vela preview/check is missing** | Current PR head, event/job association, pending status, approved VPN/operator access. | Treating unavailable evidence as passing. |
| **Protected apply times out** | Exact merge commit, correlation ID, completed steps, app/resource readback. | Replaying the protected merge or submitting another create request. |
| **Source checkout/registry fails** | Recorded source, platform-owned access binding, network/trust, package permissions. | Embedding a personal token in a URL or disabling TLS verification. |
| **Danger fails or cannot authenticate** | Rule result, current metadata, trusted threshold input, protected bot binding/API response. | Giving PR-controlled code the bot secret or issuing an ad hoc broad PAT. |
| **Android signing fails** | Approved artifact/route, alias/password binding, local-path versus URL contract, expected signer. | Regenerating the signing key as the first fix. |
| **iOS profile/signing fails** | Bundle and extension IDs, entitlements, certificate/private key, profile, team, export method, and authorized API access. | Assuming an App Store Connect role alone guarantees signing compatibility. |
| **Locked dependencies fail** | Tool versions, registry reachability, source/lock consistency, supported package versions. | Deleting lockfiles and updating everything. |
| **Sauce job fails** | Artifact/suite association, region, approved credentials, device availability, runner report, and actual assertions. | Calling app registration/upload a passing device test. |
| **NowSecure blocks** | Completed assessment, exact binary, findings, severity, and policy decision. | Suppressing findings or lowering thresholds to turn the build green. |
| **Store upload returns an error** | Exact request/result, package/version identity, permissions, destination processing, and possible accepted upload. | Uploading the same candidate again before reconciling ambiguity. |
| **User cannot see the release** | Environment, version, enablement, audience/entitlement, tester eligibility, and client state. | Assigning everyone or recreating the app as a shortcut. |

These are diagnostic starting points, not established root causes. For example, a 401 response indicates an authentication/access problem to investigate; it does not by itself prove which credential expired.

## 6. Preserve platform-specific evidence

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="Android">

Record the actual variant, application ID, version code/name, selected JDK/Gradle toolchain, artifact hash, and relevant reports. Use the existing signing verifier for approved read-only APK identity checks.

For a bounded source-check failure, rerun the actual local task only after establishing that it does not repeat an external delivery operation. For a standard debug application:

```bash
./gradlew :app:lintDebug :app:testDebugUnitTest :app:assembleDebug
```

Use real module/flavor names. Do not run a broad release workflow merely to reproduce one unit-test failure. Do not replace a release key with debug signing to obtain an artifact that appears successful.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

Record Xcode, workspace/project, scheme/configuration, bundle/version/build, extension identities, profile/signing evidence, and the actual failing action: build, archive, export, upload, processing, or downstream signing.

Safe tool identity checks on an authorized Mac:

```bash
xcode-select -p
xcodebuild -version
xcrun swift --version
```

A passed archive and failed App Management signature are different events. Do not replace certificates or remove provisioning profiles without the signing owner's investigation. An upload failure after transport may still require destination readback.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="React Native">

Record Node/package-manager versions, lockfile/source identity, Metro versus release configuration, native target, and whether the failure is JavaScript or native.

For the established npm script contract, run only the relevant reviewed local checks:

```bash
npm run typecheck
npm run lint
npm test
```

Do not regenerate `package-lock.json`, switch package managers, or delete `Podfile.lock` as a generic recovery. Keep Redux Toolkit behavior separate from native linking/build failures; investigate the boundary that actually failed.

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

Local commands are for reviewed, non-delivery checks. They do not authorize a pipeline rerun that includes provisioning, signing-service writes, uploads, or publication.

## 7. Reconcile independent route outcomes

Use the exact route and destination identity. Preserve successful work while investigating another route.

| Route state | Recovery boundary |
| :--- | :--- |
| Build failed before any external delivery | Fix the bounded cause and follow the approved build rerun procedure. Confirm no earlier external effects. |
| Build passed, upload unknown | Preserve artifact and reconcile destination receipt/version. |
| Upload confirmed, signing failed | Reconcile the existing destination object and signing operation. Do not create another version blindly. |
| Signed/enabled, audience unknown | Inspect assignment and intended-user visibility; do not repeat upload. |
| One route verified, another failed | Keep the verified route and its evidence; recover only the unresolved scope. |
| UAT not started or unverified | Report that state accurately. Do not infer UAT publication from QA. |

### Workshop case: QA iOS downstream signing

The supplied 28 October 2026 canary evidence establishes:

- App creation and private artifact inspection completed.
- QA Android distribution was signed, enabled, assigned, and user-visible.
- QA iOS Bitrise build passed; downstream App Management signing failed with `distribution_signing_failed`.
- UAT publication was not confirmed.

The safe next action was to reconcile **QA iOS** while preserving confirmed **QA Android** publication. The issue was accepted as known for that exercise; its underlying cause was not established.

Do not turn this into a universal certificate-replacement recipe. It demonstrates how to separate successful build evidence from incomplete delivery and how to preserve a successful route.

**Attach screenshot — sanitized apply summary showing completed steps, failed route, correlation identity, and safe next action.**

## 8. Treat security incidents separately from routine failures

If credentials, private keys, signed download URLs, or sensitive data were exposed, invoke the security incident process. Authorized owners assess containment, revocation/rotation, audit evidence, affected artifacts, and required remediation.

Do not copy exposed values into support reports. Deleting a file in the latest commit does not remove historical exposure. Signing identity changes require their platform/store continuity procedure; generic key regeneration can create additional damage.

NowSecure findings require evidence-backed remediation and reassessment under the applicable process. A finding is not automatically a credential exposure, and adding one security control does not guarantee every reported issue is resolved.

## 9. Recover publication without claiming binary rollback

A remote destination change may stop new distribution or select an eligible version under its supported controls; it does not automatically replace an already installed mobile binary on every device.

The supplied recording notes describe roll-forward builds with incremented versioning for production fixes. Follow the approved emergency release process and current destination constraints. Do not promise an over-the-air downgrade or use feature flags as a substitute for correcting an unsafe binary.

Any disablement, reassignment, track update, or new upload is an external mutation requiring the appropriate authorization. Preserve evidence and verify the intended user outcome afterward.

## 10. Escalate with a useful, sanitized record

Use the approved Teams support channel. Keep exact internal links/identities in the authorized record; share only what the audience is permitted to see.

```markdown
# CI/CD Recovery Request

- Application and owning team:
- Route, environment, and intended destination:
- Source branch and actual build commit:
- Configuration merge and template/version, where relevant:
- Execution and correlation references:
- First failed operation and observed error category:
- Confirmed completed steps and successful routes:
- Was an external mutation attempted? What is known?
- Read-only reconciliation performed and evidence:
- Still pending/unknown:
- Preserved resources and artifacts:
- Required owner and requested decision:
- Approved recovery action/reference, when granted:
- Recovery result and final per-route verification:
```

A support request is not approval to rerun. Record the explicit authorized next action and verify its result before closing the incident.

## 11. Test recovery behavior before relying on it

| Scenario | Expected behavior |
| :--- | :--- |
| No readback or no evidence reference | Preserve and reconcile. |
| Resource is authoritatively present | Use/reconcile the existing result rather than creating it again. |
| Resource remains pending | Bounded observation or escalation, not repeated upload. |
| Absence is non-authoritative or not conclusive | Do not authorize another write from that observation. |
| Conclusive authoritative absence without recovery approval | Request reviewed recovery. |
| Conclusive absence with validated approval | Follow only that authorized recovery scope. |
| One route already verified | Its resources and evidence remain intact. |
| Recovery attempt becomes uncertain | Stop again and reconcile; do not loop blindly. |
| Credential exposure | Incident handling occurs without spreading the secret. |

Test the decision helper with synthetic records; verify actual read-only adapters, authorization checks, and destination consistency behavior separately. Internal readback schemas, reconciliation endpoints, and authorization runbooks were not supplied in full, so no executable external API call or mutation is invented here.

**Reference checks:** MDX and five shell snippets passed syntax checks. The recovery-decision helper passed strict TypeScript checking and 10 synthetic decision cases. No vendor readback, recovery mutation, build, or signing operation was executed. Internal endpoint/authorization integration and flowchart rendering remain unverified.

## Completion checklist

- [ ] Failure is localized to an exact operation, source revision, artifact, route, and environment.
- [ ] Completed steps, successful routes, and resources are preserved.
- [ ] Unknown external writes are reconciled through approved read-only operations.
- [ ] Missing, inaccessible, and still-processing resources are not mistaken for conclusive absence.
- [ ] Recovery action has a responsible owner and reviewed authorization.
- [ ] No blind enrollment replay, upload retry, credential replacement, or deletion was performed.
- [ ] Recovery results have current destination and user-verification evidence.
- [ ] Sensitive evidence stays within approved systems.
- [ ] Remaining unknown outcomes and follow-up owners are explicit.

