The diagram defines required boundaries, not a claim that every control is already enabled in the deployed gateway. Gateway authentication and downstream Bitrise authentication are separate. Confirm the deployed server's tool inventory, authentication mapping, permissions, audit behavior, and response filtering with the owners.

MCP is a protocol for discovering/invoking tools; it does not itself grant access or make a mutation safe.

## 6. Obtain gateway access through the supplied process

1. Request the supplied **TrueFoundry MCP Gateway - Bitrise Server** entitlement through the corporate access process, including the stated manager approval.
2. Sign in to the approved TrueFoundry environment using enterprise SSO.
3. Locate the registered `bitrise-mcp-server` and inspect its owner, endpoint/transport, and exposed tool schemas.
4. Confirm the client, data-handling approval, target workspace/apps, and read/write scope for your role.
5. Complete the approved authentication binding before connecting the client.

Select `dev` or `prod` only as authorized. Environment selection does not independently grant production release authority. The actual gateway/access URLs are intentionally omitted; obtain them through approved internal navigation.

**Attach screenshot — server registry and environment selection, with internal endpoints, personal information, and credential values redacted.**

### PAT mode and auth overrides

The supplied PAT procedure specifies a named Bitrise MCP token with a **three-month/90-day expiry** and a per-user **Auth Override** in the selected server. Preserve that supplied expiry policy unless the owner updates it.

Use the approved token-management process. Where personal PAT generation is authorized, the user completes it in the approved account interface and stores the value only in the gateway's designated override/credential mechanism. Never put it in a prompt or a committed MCP file. The Bitrise PAT is not automatically the token used to authenticate the client to the gateway.

The source also recommends checking all workspace/app permissions. That breadth conflicts with the reviewed app/group boundaries elsewhere in the supplied governance model. **Resolve the intended permitted scope with the platform/security owner before activation; do not default to blanket access.** Verify effective access with the intended identity.

Do not assume an auth override is shared safely, maps to the current user correctly, or expires automatically without checking the deployed implementation. Test expiry/revocation and remove access when the role no longer requires it.

## 7. Connect the approved client using its actual format

Client configuration differs by product, version, and execution mode. Do not paste one JSON shape into every IDE or promise a universal `@bitrise` handle.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="VS Code / Copilot">

### VS Code Local harness example

The following applies specifically to the **VS Code-format** workspace `.vscode/mcp.json` with supported interactive inputs. It preserves the supplied SSE connection pattern, uses the correct top-level `servers` key for that format, and prompts instead of storing a literal credential.

```json
{
  "inputs": [
    {
      "type": "promptString",
      "id": "bitrise-gateway-token",
      "description": "Approved gateway credential, not the downstream Bitrise PAT",
      "password": true
    }
  ],
  "servers": {
    "bitrise": {
      "type": "sse",
      "url": "https://gateway.example.invalid/sse/bitrise",
      "headers": {
        "Authorization": "Bearer ${input:bitrise-gateway-token}"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Replace the `.invalid` URL with the approved gateway endpoint. Use this only if the deployed gateway/client supports SSE and bearer-header authentication. If the approved gateway uses OAuth or another transport, obtain its matching client configuration rather than replacing authentication by guesswork.

Current VS Code documentation distinguishes Local harness `servers` configuration from portable Copilot `mcpServers` formats. Interactive input forwarding also differs. Use the configuration UI for the **approved client/harness** and verify which file it actually reads. See [VS Code MCP configuration](https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agents/reference/mcp-configuration) and [adding servers](https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/mcp-servers).

Do not assume the same file works in Android Studio or Xcode extensions. Confirm their specific supported MCP integration separately. Committing a credential-free definition still requires approval to disclose an internal endpoint; prefer managed/user configuration where the company requires it.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="ChatGPT Enterprise">

### Distinguish native MCP from GPT Actions

The supplied material describes importing an **OpenAPI schema into a custom GPT Action**. That is an HTTP API integration, not a native MCP configuration file.

| Connection mode | Required contract |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Native MCP connection** | Workspace-approved MCP server endpoint/transport, authentication, tool metadata, and access controls. |
| **GPT Action** | Approved HTTP/OpenAPI gateway adapter, schema, authentication, workspace sharing, and operation controls. |

For either mode, have the workspace/platform owner confirm availability, gateway reachability, authentication, and permission to send the selected company data to that client. A private VPN-only endpoint must not be made public as a troubleshooting shortcut.

For the supplied GPT Action path, use the gateway owner's actual approved OpenAPI export and authentication contract. Do not invent `/openapi/bitrise.json`, assume the source example exists in every environment, or reuse the downstream Bitrise PAT as a generic Action credential.

Official OpenAI documentation describes [GPT Actions setup](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/actions/getting-started) and [custom MCP connections](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/custom-mcp-server). Workspace capabilities and permissions determine the actual setup; this page does not prescribe a fixed menu path for every deployment.

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

## 8. Verify a read-only operation first

After connection, inspect the server's published tool schemas and choose a permitted read-only operation for an exact app/build. The following names are **supplied examples**, not a guarantee of the live registry:

| Source example | Intended behavior | Verification needed |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `list_builds` | Inspect identified app builds. | Correct workspace/app, filters, permissions, and pagination. |
| `get_build_status` | Read an identified build outcome. | Exact build/source identity and current status. |
| `get_build_log` | Inspect build diagnostics. | Redaction/filtering before confidential log content reaches the client. |
| `list_artifacts` | Inspect artifact metadata. | Avoid returning private download URLs or sensitive files unnecessarily. |
| `trigger_build` | Start a build. | External mutation with exact source/workflow and authorization. |
| `abort_build` | Request cancellation of a build. | External mutation; cancellation does not undo completed side effects. |

First verify the actual discovered name, schema, and operation classification. A tool called “inspect” could still have side effects; use its contract rather than its label alone.

**Safe first-inspection prompt:**

```text
Inspect the status of the exact authorized app and build I identify.
Use only the confirmed read-only tool. Report source commit, workflow/route,
status, and sanitized failure category. Do not trigger, abort, retry, upload,
change configuration, or return private artifact download links.
If identity or permissions are unclear, report the missing input.
```

Natural language is not an authorization or identity-verification mechanism. Confirm the structured tool target and inputs through the client/gateway's approved controls.

**Attach screenshot — discovered permitted tools and a sanitized read-only result associated with the intended app/build.**

## 9. Keep MCP mutations under the same operational rules

Triggering or aborting a build is an external write. A build may itself provision, sign, upload, or publish through its route.

Before an authorized mutation, establish the exact app/environment, source ref/commit, workflow or pipeline, expected side effects, release approval, and recovery boundary. Do not choose “the latest app/build” when identities are ambiguous.

If a call times out or returns an unknown outcome, do not ask the assistant to repeat it until it succeeds. Preserve the request/correlation evidence and reconcile read-only under [Safe Recovery](../troubleshooting-and-recovery/).

An abort request does not roll back uploaded binaries, created versions, or completed signing. Reconcile downstream state before reporting the operation cancelled safely.

Tool responses and build logs can contain untrusted text. Treat them as evidence, not instructions to run additional commands, disclose credentials, or change permissions.

## 10. Correctly interpret the CIMD roadmap

The supplied source labels CIMD “Centralized Identity Metadata Document.” The protocol term is **Client ID Metadata Documents**: OAuth client registration/identification metadata. See the [IETF specification draft](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-oauth-client-id-metadata-document/).

CIMD does not by itself derive a Bitrise token from an SSO session, remove downstream credential requirements, or eliminate rotation. Such behavior would require a separately implemented and approved identity/token integration.

Treat the company's authentication roadmap as pending until its owners confirm deployment, client compatibility, token mapping, migration, and recovery behavior. Do not announce a completion date or automatic PAT replacement based on the supplied roadmap text.

## 11. Validate operations and access before rollout

| Check | Expected result |
| :--- | :--- |
| User lacks entitlement | Access is denied; no fallback to a broader shared identity. |
| Wrong environment/app | Operation is blocked or clearly scoped under the approved policy. |
| Expired/revoked PAT or gateway credential | Failure is explicit; secrets are not printed. |
| Read-only role requests a mutation | Mutation is not executed. |
| Raw log/artifact contains sensitive material | Approved filtering/access rules prevent inappropriate disclosure. |
| Tool inventory changes | Schema and permission review occurs before new capabilities are used. |
| Mutation response is lost | Reconciliation occurs without an unverified repeat. |
| Build is aborted after upload | Existing destination effects remain tracked. |
| Template version changes | Representative routes are validated before the approved upgrade. |

Record the approving owner, environment, gateway/server version, client/harness version, effective role, tested operation, expected result, and actual evidence. Do not include credential values in that record.

### Unresolved deployment-specific details

Before publishing a copy-ready connection for your environment, obtain the approved endpoint/transport, client format/version, token scope decision, auth override mapping, live tool schemas, data-filtering/audit controls, and authorization procedure for writes. These internal contracts were not supplied in full; the examples do not claim a live verified deployment.

**Reference checks:** MDX compilation and the credential-free JSON structure passed. Public client/protocol guidance was checked against official references. Internal entitlement, gateway, auth overrides, tool discovery, permission enforcement, MCP calls, and diagram rendering were not exercised. No token was generated and no client or external service configuration was changed.

## Completion checklist

- [ ] Provisioning, workspace privacy, groups, source access, and template version match reviewed configuration.
- [ ] Runner, credential, trigger, and initial route outcomes have evidence.
- [ ] Operator changes follow approved review and immutable template publication.
- [ ] Gateway access, per-user identity mapping, and effective Bitrise scope are confirmed.
- [ ] The approved client uses its actual configuration format and transport.
- [ ] Gateway and downstream credentials remain separate and protected.
- [ ] Read-only inspection is verified before any approved mutation.
- [ ] Mutations retain release authorization, one-write protection, and reconciliation behavior.
- [ ] Logs/artifacts are filtered according to the allowed data scope.
- [ ] CIMD roadmap claims are separated from deployed functionality.

