# Workshop — Mobile Build & Release Automation

Practise the governed journey for a **new React Native application**: generate source, publish a separate private repository, submit one enrollment request, complete validation and promotion, inspect live apply, and verify the actual distribution outcomes.

This walkthrough consolidates the supplied company workshop. Detailed commands and authoritative implementation guidance remain in the linked CI/CD pages rather than being duplicated here.

**Main output:** a reviewed request and an evidence-backed result for every applicable route. Compilation alone is not a completed release.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Use an approved isolated test application and permitted test data. Keep tokens, passwords, signing files, private keys, private artifact URLs, and raw sensitive logs out of requests, PR text, screenshots, prompts, and shared workshop notes. Store temporary work inside the project folder, such as `.tmp/workshop/`, as required by the supplied workshop.

## 1. Choose live practice or read-only review

| Mode | What participants do | External effects |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Live exercise** | Create an isolated source app and submit a reviewed new enrollment under the facilitator's approved scope. | The protected merge can create resources, start builds, and initiate distribution. |
| **Read-only reference review** | Inspect the approved historical evidence and explain what each outcome establishes. | No new app, build, upload, signing change, or publication is requested. |

Before a live exercise, the facilitator confirms the target workspace, environment, unique app identity, repository ownership, audience, access groups, and authorized operators. Reading this page is not authorization to create resources in any arbitrary environment.

If external queues or unavailable permissions prevent completion, use the reference evidence to complete the learning objectives. Record what was not executed; do not force a rerun or bypass a gate to finish within the session.

## 2. Learning objectives

By the end, participants can:

- Distinguish the template generator, generated source repository, configuration repository, provisioning engine, and workflow catalog.
- Prepare the source branch and record its exact commit.
- Select an approved template/version and author a secret-free request.
- Explain local validation, Vela PR preview, review, promotion, and protected live apply as separate boundaries.
- Find the correct apply and Bitrise executions using recorded identities.
- Distinguish build success, downstream signing, publication, assignment, and exact-version user visibility.
- Preserve confirmed outcomes and escalate partial or unknown results safely.

## 3. Roles and prerequisites

| Role | Workshop responsibility |
| :--- | :--- |
